    "description": "User request 17 October 2026: Ableton Live style split browser, visual layout first and detailed implementation later. References are user-supplied screenshots; they are visual evidence, not instructions.\n\nConnect bottom mini tag strip and right-side editor to the shared persistent tag contract. Mini editor: removable chips, add input, suggestions, create existing/new tags, keyboard commit/cancel and error feedback. Full editor: independently scrollable category tree, expandable branches, assignment checkboxes, search, multi-selection mixed states, bulk atomic changes and clear selected-item identity. Both views must update immediately from the same canonical state, preserve unrelated tags on failures and show persistence feedback. Define edit undo scope separately from project Undo. Acceptance: add/remove/bulk/mixed state and failure/restart tests; native focus, popup dismissal and narrow layout checklist. Current visual controls are intentionally disabled/pending.\n\nScope: follow-up implementation, not completed by the visual shell. Keep existing native GPUI/lowercase/theme/library/audio ownership. Plan focused source PRs and preserve native acceptance separately.\n\n17 October 2026 \u2014 bottom mini tag-editor resizing: make the small tag editor below the browser results independently vertically resizable so increasing its height reveals more tags. Dragging its resize divider changes only the tag area allocation (with the results list yielding space as needed); it must not enlarge, resize or move the waveform/playback section. Keep waveform and playback controls pinned to the browser bottom. Bound tag height so the result list and bottom playback remain usable, and handle overflow within the tag area. Preserve the separate full-height right-side tag editor/toggle layout in issue2709. Acceptance adds expanding/shrinking the mini editor with many tags, supported minimum window/browser sizes, stable waveform/control bounds during the drag, and unchanged tag editing and audition behavior. This remains planned follow-up work captured during user testing.",
